      "detail": "Friday 26 July. Fine, warm, no wind.\n\n8.00 pm — Dinner. Deceased, Mr. J. Penhallow (nephew), Mrs. C. Penhallow, Dr. M. Ellery, Mr. E. Kittow (solicitor), Miss A. Quill (companion). Words between deceased and nephew over a new will.\n9.30 pm — Deceased locks herself in glasshouse to watch her orchid flower. Says she will ring the bell once when it opens, and again when the household may come down to see it.\n10.15 pm — Miss Quill takes her cocoa.\n10.30 pm — Mrs. C. Penhallow retires with a headache.\n11.32 pm — Glasshouse bell. Deceased seen from terrace beside her flower, lamp in hand, by Mr. J. Penhallow, Mr. Kittow, Miss Quill and Dr. Ellery. Lamp lowered c. 11.35.\n11.45 pm — Mrs. C. Penhallow comes down to drawing room.\n12.15 am — Nephew seen on lawn going towards glasshouse (Miss Quill, from her window).\n5.45 am — J. Trevenna, gardener, finds deceased at foot of stepladder below orchid bench. Glasshouse door locked inside, key in lock. Stokehole door unbolted.\n\nDr. Tresidder: fell from the steps and struck her head on the iron staging. Wound above left ear, round, size of a half-crown. Death between 11.30 pm and 2 am.\n\nNephew had motive, was out alone after midnight, and the stokehole was open. — Sgt. W. Polglase"
